== Design

The Design phase builds security into the product architecture. Its controls run throughout active development — revisit them whenever the design evolves or new components are introduced.

.*What this phase covers*
[%collapsible]
====
[mermaid]
----
flowchart TB
    S[Scoping] --> C[Security context]
    C --> T[Threats and risks]
    T --> R[Security requirements]
    C --> D[Design reviews]
    T --> D
    C --> E[External technologies and suppliers]
----

* *Scoping* — decide which secure development activities apply to this project, based on its intended use and risk profile.
* *Security context* — capture the intended use, misuse scenarios, essential functions and environmental assumptions, so that later work has a shared basis.
* *Threats and risks* — model the architecture, identify design weaknesses, and track the cybersecurity risks that follow from them.
* *Security requirements* — turn context, threats and risks into requirements that implementation and verification can be held to.
* *Design reviews* — check the design against secure design practice and against privacy obligations.
* *External technologies and suppliers* — assess third-party technologies, justify cryptographic choices, and ensure that suppliers and subprojects develop securely.

The controls listed below are the authoritative scope of this phase. Installed extension modules may add further controls here.
====

=== Why it matters

Fixing security weaknesses in design is far less costly than fixing them in production. Threat modeling and security requirements give the team a shared understanding of what needs protection and how — reducing surprises later in verification and operations.

